    <section id="cs-situation">
        <h3>The situation</h3>
        <p>By 2024 the agency had a working chat tool and a handful of agents. Image workflows and other experiments were running in half a dozen teams. The projects worked, but each one had a separate login and its own copy of client information. Teams also set their own rules about who could see what, so every new idea started from zero.</p>
        <p>That approach stopped being manageable as the number of tools grew. The agency also held brand guidelines and strategy, plus research and unreleased work for companies that often competed with one another. Before an AI system could be useful, it had to know whose data it was handling and who was allowed to use it.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-role">
        <h3>What I owned</h3>
        <p>I owned product and technical direction for Nexus within the five-person product and engineering group I led. I defined the platform boundary, the access model for knowledge and agents, and the capabilities that became shared services. I directed the build sequence and worked across UX, data science, and transformation to move the architecture into adopted products.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-foundation">
        <h3>01 &middot; Foundation: Nexus</h3>
        <p>Models and applications change quickly. Access controls and trusted organizational context keep their value. We designed Nexus around those basics so new products could inherit them from the start.</p>
        <p><strong>Identity and access.</strong> SSO and OAuth 2.0 handled authentication. Organization and user management applied org-scoped permissions during retrieval. An application could only reach data it had been granted, and no team had to rebuild those controls.</p>
        <p><strong>Shared services.</strong> Teams could register org-scoped agents with Nexus and call common AI services from their own products. Those services covered retrieval and generation, including tool use.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-adoption">
        <h3>02 &middot; Adoption: Mod Chat to Nexus Chat</h3>
        <p>Nexus Chat was where most people met the platform. It grew out of Mod Chat and became a model-agnostic harness. At request time it connected the selected model with available tools, governed knowledge, and an org-scoped agent. Workflows and agent linking handled repeated or multi-step tasks, while the model itself could change without forcing the rest of the system to move.</p>
        <p class="metrics">As of July 2026, <strong>80%</strong> of the agency had used it and more than <strong>60%</strong> were active in a given month. Users and agents had moved through <strong>27,000+ conversations</strong>. Retention started around <strong>40%</strong> immediately after onboarding and climbed with cohort tenure to more than <strong>90% monthly retention</strong> among the longest-term users.</p>
        <p>The retention curve mattered more to me than the launch number. A mandate and a training session can produce a busy first month. Here, established cohorts used the product more consistently over time, evidence that it had become part of how they worked.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-context">
        <h3>03 &middot; Context: Knowledge Layers + Brand Agents</h3>
        <p>Knowledge Layers stored approved material for companies and clients, down to a specific brand or project. Brand Agents were persistent assistants grounded in that governed context, not personalities wrapped around a system prompt. More than <strong>200 Brand Agents</strong> were deployed across agency and client work.</p>
        <p>By April 2026, an MCP server exposed six Nexus tools. They covered organization discovery and activity, along with the core knowledge-layer operations. MCP-compatible environments such as Claude Code and Cursor could use authorized context from <strong>255+ organizations</strong> and <strong>393+ knowledge layers</strong> inside the tools where people already worked.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-productization">
        <h3>04 &middot; Packaging: Orion</h3>
        <p>Once the underlying system was real, it needed a name and a shape that people outside the product team could understand. Orion grouped the internal platform and client offers into one connected-intelligence ecosystem. I owned the six-product portfolio it organized and helped define the story used internally and with clients: Nexus, Brand Agents, Mod Heat, Audience Lab, GEO by Mod Op, and Launchpad.</p>
        <p>Each product used the same foundation differently. Launchpad moved early prototypes toward governed deployment. Audience Lab became a new strategy and measurement offer. <a href="/geo">GEO</a> was the first product designed as a direct offer, connecting a free audit to a paid program. Mod Heat, the first product we released, showed early on that brand-specific context could make an AI workflow useful.</p>
        <p class="metrics">Internal modeling estimated roughly <strong>$5.4M</strong> in value since the March 2025 launch, based on about <strong>48,000 human-hours</strong> of work produced by users and agents. Those figures were modeled labor-value equivalents, not booked revenue. <a href="https://digiday.com/marketing/build-or-buy-inside-indie-agency-mod-ops-ai-infrastructure/" target="_blank">Digiday covered the build-versus-buy decision</a> in August 2026 and cited roughly $3M in avoided licensing costs across Orion.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-changed">
        <h3>What changed</h3>
        <p>New products inherited identity, permissions, and approved context from day one. Product and engineering planning now ran alongside adoption and commercial planning. Visible products made continued investment in the platform easier to justify.</p>
    </section>

